# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI Dashboard — environment configuration
# Copy to .env.local for local development. All values are optional in Phase 1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the SQLite database file lives.
# Local default: ./data/dashboard.db
# Railway: point this at a mounted volume, e.g. /data/dashboard.db
DATABASE_PATH=./data/dashboard.db

# Where uploaded project files are written (Phase 3).
# Railway: use the same volume, e.g. /data/uploads
UPLOADS_DIR=./data/uploads

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Access control
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required for any deployment reachable over a network. Without it, a production
# server refuses to serve the dashboard rather than exposing it — the browser
# will prompt for this password (any username).
#
# Not needed for the desktop app, which runs on 127.0.0.1, or for `npm run dev`.
DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub integration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 1/2: a Personal Access Token is the fastest path to REAL repository data.
# Create one at https://github.com/settings/tokens with the `repo` scope
# (or `public_repo` if you only want public repositories).
# When this is unset, the dashboard runs on a clearly-labelled demo provider.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Enables "Connect with GitHub" in Settings, which authorises through GitHub's
# device flow — no token to create, no permissions to choose.
#
# Create an OAuth App at https://github.com/settings/developers:
#   * Application name and homepage URL can be anything.
#   * Authorization callback URL is required by the form but unused; put
#     http://localhost
#   * Tick "Enable Device Flow" — without it, GitHub rejects the request.
# Copy the Client ID below. No client secret is needed: the device flow does not
# use one, which is why it is safe to ship in a desktop application.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
